To develop a custom WordPress plugin that adds a specific functionality to an existing website, follow these steps:

Step 1: Plan and Identify the Custom Functionality
Before creating a plugin, plan and identify the specific functionality that you want to implement on the website. Decide on the new custom post type, new widget, and shortcodes that you need to implement.

For example, let's assume you want to create a "Portfolio" custom post type on the website, a new "Recent Portfolio" widget, and two shortcodes: `[portfolio]` for displaying a grid of recent portfolio items and `[single_portfolio]` for displaying a single portfolio item.

Step 2: Set Up a New WordPress Plugin
Create a new WordPress plugin by naming it "My Portfolio Plugin" and saving the file with the .php extension.

```
Plugin Name: My Portfolio Plugin
Plugin URI: https://www.example.com/portfolio-plugin
Description: My Portfolio Plugin is a custom WordPress plugin that adds a Portfolio custom post type, Recent Portfolio widget, and two shortcodes (portfolio and single_portfolio).
Version: 1.0
Author: Your Name
Author URI: https://www.example.com/
License: GPL2
Text Domain: my-portfolio-plugin
Domain Path: /languages
```

Step 3: Create the Custom Post Type
In the plugin file, create a new custom post type functionality for the Portfolio post type. You can use the `register_post_type()` function to define new post types.

```
function my_portfolio_post_type() {
  $args = array(
    'public' => true,
    'labels' => array(
      'name' => 'Portfolio',
      'singular_name' => 'Portfolio',
      'add_new_item' => 'Add New Portfolio'
    ),
    'menu_icon' => 'dashicons-format-gallery',
    'menu_position' => 5,
    'supports' => array('thumbnail')
  );
  register_post_type('portfolio', $args);
}
add_action('init', 'my_portfolio_post_type');
```

This code will create a new Portfolio custom post type that enables the thumbnail feature, and adds its icon and position to the admin menu.

Step 4: Add New Widget
Create a new widget that displays the recent Portfolio items. You can use the `WP_Widget` class to define the widget.

```
class My_Widget_Portfolio extends WP_Widget {

  function __construct() {
    parent::__construct(
      'my_widget_portfolio',
      'Recent Portfolio',
      array( 'description' => 'Displays recent Portfolio items' )
    );
  }

  public function widget( $args, $instance ) {
    global $post;

    // Only display widget on Portfolio archive page
    if( is_post_type_archive('portfolio') ) {

      $args = array(
        'post_type' => 'portfolio',
        'posts_per_page' => $instance['portfolio_recent_count'],
        'no_found_rows' => true,
        'orderby' => 'date',
        'order' => 'DESC'
      );

      $portfolio_recent = new WP_Query( $args );

      if( $portfolio_recent->have_posts() ) : 

        echo $args['before_widget'];

        foreach( $portfolio_recent->posts as $portfolio_post ) : setup_postdata( $portfolio_post ); 

        ?>
        <a href="<?php the_permalink(); ?>"><?php the_post_thumbnail(); ?></a>
        <?php
        endforeach;

        echo $args['after_widget'];

        wp_reset_postdata();

      endif;

      wp_reset_query();

    }
  }

  public function form( $instance ) {
    if ( isset( $instance['portfolio_recent_count'] ) ) {
      $portfolio_recent_count = $instance['portfolio_recent_count'];
    } else {
      $portfolio_recent_count = __( '5', 'my-portfolio-plugin' );
    }
    ?>
    <p>
      <label for="<?php echo $this->get_field_id( 'portfolio_recent_count' ); ?>"><?php _e( 'Number of Recent Items:' ); ?></label>
      <input class="widefat" id="<?php echo $this->get_field_id( 'portfolio_recent_count' ); ?>" name="<?php echo $this->get_field_name( 'portfolio_recent_count' ); ?>" type="number" min="1" max="10" step="1" value="<?php echo esc_attr( $portfolio_recent_count ); ?>">
    </p>
    <?php
  }

  public function update( $new_instance, $old_instance ) {
    $instance = array();
    $instance['portfolio_recent_count'] = ( ! empty( $new_instance['portfolio_recent_count'] ) ) ? strip_tags( $new_instance['portfolio_recent_count'] ) : '';
    return $instance;
  }
}
register_widget( 'My_Widget_Portfolio' );
```

This code creates a new widget called Recent Portfolio, displays a grid of the most recent Portfolio items, and offers an option for customizing the number of Portfolio items displayed in the widget.

Step 5: Add Shortcodes
Create two custom shortcodes, `[portfolio]` and `[single_portfolio]`, that display a grid of recent Portfolio items and a single Portfolio item respectively.

Create a new file named `my-portfolio-shortcodes.php` in the `includes` folder of your plugin.

```
<?php
/**
 * Custom Shortcodes
 */

function my_portfolio_shortcode_grid( $atts ) {

  global $post;

  // Only display shortcode on Portfolio archive page
  if( is_post_type_archive('portfolio') ) {

    $a = shortcode_atts( array(
      'count' => 5 // Default value
    ), $atts );

    $args = array(
      'post_type' => 'portfolio',
      'posts_per_page' => $a['count'],
      'no_found_rows' => true,
      'orderby' => 'date',
      'order' => 'DESC'
    );

    $portfolio_recent = new WP_Query( $args );

    $output = '';

    if( $portfolio_recent->have_posts() ) : 

      $output .= '<div class="grid">';
      foreach( $portfolio_recent->posts as $portfolio_post ) : setup_postdata( $portfolio_post ); 

      ?>
      <div class="grid-item">
        <a href="<?php the_permalink(); ?>"><?php the_post_thumbnail(); ?></a>
      </div>
      <?php
      endforeach;

      $output .= '</div>';

      wp_reset_postdata();

    endif;

    wp_reset_query();

    return $output;

  }

}
add_shortcode( 'portfolio', 'my_portfolio_shortcode_grid' );

function my_portfolio_shortcode_single( $atts ) {

  global $post;

  // Only display shortcode on single Portfolio page
  if( is_singular('portfolio') ) {

    $output = '';

    ?>
    <div class="single-portfolio">
      <div class="entry-thumbnail"><?php the_post_thumbnail(); ?></div>
      <div class="entry-content">
        <?php the_content(); ?>
      </div>
    </div>
    <?php

    return $output;

  }

}
