To develop a custom WordPress plugin for adding a specific functionality to an existing website, follow the guidelines below:

1. Define the plugin's features - Based on the requirements, create a list of features that your plugin will provide. For this example, let's assume the following:

   - Custom post type: "Services"
   - New widget: "Recent Services"
   - New shortcode: "[services listing]"

2. Choose a development environment - Set up a development environment with the latest version of WordPress. Recommended tools include:

   - Local installation: XAMPP, MAMP, WampServer, or similar tools
   - Code editor: Visual Studio Code, Sublime Text, or other powerful text editors
   - Git: To manage version control and streamline development workflows

3. Create a new plugin file - Use a code editor to create a new PHP file with the following structure:

   ```
   <?php
   /*
   Plugin Name: Custom Services Plugin
   Description: Add custom "Services" post type, "Recent Services" widget, and "[services listing]" shortcode to your website.
   Version: 1.0.0
   Author: [Your Name]
   Author URI: [Your Website]
   Text Domain: custom-services
   Domain Path: /languages
   */
   ```

4. Organize the plugin's structure - Create a new folder in your plugin's root directory called "inc" (or any other name you prefer). This folder will hold the plugin's functional code:

   ```
   /custom-services.php
   /inc/
     - classes.php
     - shortcodes.php
     - widgets.php
   ```

5. Create the custom post type - In the "classes.php" file, create a new class for your custom post type:

   ```
   <?php
   /**
    * Custom Post Type Class
    */
   class Custom_Post_Type {

     function __construct() {
       add_action( 'init', array( $this, 'custom_post_type' ) );
     }

     function custom_post_type() {
       $args = array(
         'label'                 => __( 'Services', 'custom-services' ),
         'singular_name'        => __( 'Service', 'custom-services' ),
         'public'                => true,
         'menu_icon'             => 'dashicons-welcome-write-blog',
         'supports'              => array( 'title', 'editor', 'thumbnail' ),
       );
       register_post_type( 'services', $args );
     }
   }

   $custom_post_type = new Custom_Post_Type();
   ```

6. Register the new widget - In the "widgets.php" file, create a new Widget class:

   ```
   <?php
   /**
    * Recent Services Widget Class
    */
   class Recent_Services_Widget extends WP_Widget {

     function __construct() {
       parent::__construct(
         'recent-services',
         __( 'Recent Services' ),
         array( 'description' => __( 'List recent Services' ) )
       );
     }

     function widget( $args, $instance ) {
       // Get the number of recent services to show
       $count = $instance['count'] ? $instance['count'] : 5;

       // Prepare the widget output
       $output = '<ul>';

       // Get the most recent services
       $services = new WP_Query( array(
         'post_type' => 'services',
         'posts_per_page' => $count,
         'orderby' => 'date',
         'order' => 'DESC'
       ) );

       if ( $services->have_posts() ) {
         while ( $services->have_posts() ) {
           $services->the_post(); 
           $output .= '<li>';
           // Add your own code to display the service's details here
           $output .= esc_html( get_the_title() );
           $output .= '</li>';
         }
         wp_reset_postdata();
       } else {
         $output .= '<li>' . __( 'No Services found.' ) . '</li>';
       }

       $output .= '</ul>';

       echo $args['before_widget'];
       echo $output;
       echo $args['after_widget'];
     }
   }
   register_widget( 'Recent_Services_Widget' );
   ```

7. Add the shortcode functionality - In the "shortcodes.php" file, create a new function to handle the shortcode:

   ```
   <?php
   /**
    * Shortcodes Class
    */
   class Custom_Shortcodes {

     public function __construct() {
       add_shortcode( 'services_listing', array( $this, 'services_listing_shortcode' ) );
     }

     function services_listing_shortcode( $atts = [] ) {
       // Customize the shortcode output here
       ob_start();
       $args = [
         'post_type' => 'services',
         'orderby' => 'date',
         'order' => 'DESC',
         'posts_per_page' => 10
       ];

       $services = new WP_Query( $args );

       if ( $services->have_posts() ) :
         while ( $services->have_posts() ) :
           $services->the_post();
           // Add your own code to display the service's details here
           echo '<p>';
           echo esc_html( get_the_title() );
           echo '</p>';
         endwhile;
       endif;

       wp_reset_postdata();
       return ob_get_clean();
     }
   }

   $custom_shortcodes = new Custom_Shortcodes();
   ```

8. Write unit tests - To ensure the plugin functionality, write unit tests for your custom post type, widget, and shortcode.

9. Style the plugin - Add WordPress styles to visually style your plugin when it is being used on your website. You can do this by creating a new stylesheet in your plugin's root directory, using the following code:

   ```
   <style>
     .services-post {
       /* Add your own styles here */
     }
     .recent-services-widget li {
       /* Add your own styles here */
     }
   </style>
   ```

10. Test and publish - Test the plugin thoroughly, making sure it is functioning as intended. Once it has been tested, package the plugin for publishing through the official WordPress plugin repository.

Additional tips:

- Use WordPress coding standards to ensure consistency.
- Utilize hooks and filters to integrate with other plugins and WordPress features.
- Document each feature and usage in a README file or in the WordPress plugin repo's documentation.
- Provide plugins translations using `gettext` and include POT files in the languages folder for translation purposes.
- Follow best practices including security, sanitization, and proper code maintenance.

Para modificar la tabla AlarmHistory en MariaDB para cumplir con los requisitos de almacenamiento mencionados, puedes crear una nueva tabla y llamarla AlarmHistory_Security. Luego, realiza los siguientes pasos:

1. Copia la definición de la tabla AlarmHistory a una nueva tabla usando el comando sql.

```sql
CREATE TABLE AlarmHistory_Security (
  -- Define las columnas de la tabla AlarmHistory_Security aquí. Debes definir solo las columnas necesarias para seguir los requisitos especificados: HistoryAction, InsertTimestamp (timestamp), UpdateTimestamp (timestamp) y DeleteTimestamp (timestamp).
);
```

2. Crea un trigger que copie los registros de INSERT y UPDATE a la tabla AlarmHistory_Security, pero asegúrate de insertar solo la última actualización.

```sql
CREATE TRIGGER insert_update_history_security
AFTER INSERT ON AlarmHistory
FOR EACH ROW
BEGIN
  IF INSERTING THEN
    INSERT INTO AlarmHistory_Security (HistoryAction, InsertTimestamp)
      VALUES ('INSERT', NOW());
  END IF;

  IF (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM AlarmHistory WHERE AlarmId = NEW.AlarmId AND UpdateTimestamp > (SELECT MAX(UpdateTimestamp) FROM AlarmHistory_Security WHERE AlarmId = NEW.AlarmId)) > 0 THEN -- último registro de actualización
    INSERT INTO AlarmHistory_Security (HistoryAction, UpdateTimestamp, AlarmId)
      VALUES ('UPDATE', NOW(), NEW.AlarmId);
  END IF;
END;

CREATE TRIGGER update_delete_history_security
AFTER UPDATE ON AlarmHistory
FOR EACH ROW
BEGIN
  DECLARE prevData AlarmHistory;
  DECLARE prevActionState VARCHAR(20);

  SELECT * INTO prevData FROM AlarmHistory WHERE AlarmId = OLD.AlarmId ORDER BY UpdateTimestamp DESC LIMIT 1;
  SELECT HistoryAction INTO prevActionState FROM AlarmHistory_Security WHERE AlarmId = OLD.AlarmId ORDER BY UpdateTimestamp DESC LIMIT 1;

  IF prevActionState = 'INSERT' THEN -- necesitamos leer el history insert para poder actualizalrlo
    UPDATE AlarmHistory_Security SET UpdateTimestamp = NOW() WHERE AlarmId = OLD.AlarmId AND HistoryAction = 'INSERT';
  END IF;

  INSERT INTO AlarmHistory_Security (HistoryAction, UpdateTimestamp, DeleteTimestamp, AlarmId)
    VALUES ('UPDATE', NOW(), OLD.DeleteTimestamp, OLD.AlarmId);
END;
```

3. Crea otro trigger que copie los registros de DELETE a la tabla AlarmHistory_Security.

```sql
CREATE TRIGGER delete_history_security
AFTER DELETE ON AlarmHistory
FOR EACH ROW
BEGIN
  INSERT INTO AlarmHistory_Security (HistoryAction, DeleteTimestamp, AlarmId)
    VALUES ('DELETE', OLD.DeleteTimestamp, OLD.AlarmId);
END;
```

Con estos tres triggers, la tabla AlarmHistory_Security contendrá:

- Un registro de INSERT con el timestamp de inserción
- Solo el último registro de UPDATE con su timestamp de actualización
- El registro de DELETE con su timestamp de borrado

Estas operaciones te permitirán optimizar tu consulta y minimizar el impacto en la velocidad de la tabla de seguridad.

Recuerda renombrar la tabla original como necesites para conservar la seguridad de tu base de datos original. Después de que todo esté configurado y probado, puedes modificar tus consultas para hacer uso de la tabla AlarmHistory_Security en lugar de la tabla original.

Para alcanzar el objetivo que describiste, podrías seguir los siguientes pasos:

1. Cree una nueva tabla en MariaDB llamada `AlarmHistorySummary` con los siguientes campos:

```
CREATE TABLE AlarmHistorySummary (
  id INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,
  alarmId INT NOT NULL,
  historyAction ENUM('INSERT', 'UPDATE', 'DELETE') NOT NULL,
  creationTime TIMESTAMP DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,
  updateTime TIMESTAMP,
  deleteTime TIMESTAMP
);
```

2. Agregue un trigger en MariaDB para la tabla `AlarmHistory` que controle y actualice la tabla de resumen:

```
CREATE TRIGGER update_summary_table
AFTER INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON AlarmHistory
FOR EACH ROW
BEGIN
  DECLARE alarmId INT;
  DECLARE action ENUM('INSERT', 'UPDATE', 'DELETE');
  DECLARE updateTime TIMESTAMP;
  DECLARE deleteTime TIMESTAMP;

  -- Get alarm ID
  SELECT id INTO alarmId FROM AlarmHistory WHERE AlarmHistory.id = NEW.id;

  -- Get action type
  IF (NEW.action IS NULL) THEN
    SET action = 'INSERT';
  ELSEIF (OLD.action IS NULL AND NEW.action IS NOT NULL) THEN
    SET action = 'UPDATE';
  ELSEIF (OLD.action IS NOT NULL AND NEW.action IS NOT NULL) THEN
    IF (OLD.action = NEW.action) THEN
      SELECT updateTime INTO updateTime FROM AlarmHistory WHERE id = NEW.id ORDER BY updateTime DESC LIMIT 1;
      SET action = 'UPDATE';
    ELSE
      SET action = 'UPDATE';
    END IF;
  ELSE
    SET action = 'DELETE';
    SELECT deleteTime INTO deleteTime FROM AlarmHistory WHERE id = NEW.id;
  END IF;

  -- Insert, update or delete entries in AlarmHistorySummary
  IF (action = 'INSERT') THEN
    INSERT INTO AlarmHistorySummary (alarmId, historyAction, creationTime) VALUES (alarmId, action, NEW.creationTime);
  ELSIF (action = 'UPDATE') THEN
    UPDATE AlarmHistorySummary
      SET updateTime = updateTime
      WHERE alarmId = alarmId AND historyAction = 'UPDATE';
    IF (deleteTime IS NOT NULL) THEN
      DELETE FROM AlarmHistorySummary
        WHERE alarmId = alarmId AND historyAction = 'DELETE' AND deleteTime < updateTime;
    END IF;
    INSERT INTO AlarmHistorySummary (alarmId, historyAction, updateTime) VALUES (alarmId, action, updateTime);
  ELSIF (action = 'DELETE') THEN
    UPDATE AlarmHistorySummary
      SET deleteTime = deleteTime
      WHERE alarmId = alarmId AND historyAction = 'DELETE';
    INSERT INTO AlarmHistorySummary (alarmId, historyAction, deleteTime) VALUES (alarmId, action, deleteTime);
  END IF;
END;
```

3. Actualice los scripts de inserción, actualización, y eliminación de registros en la tabla `AlarmHistory` para usar los siguientes formatos:

Para `INSERT`:

```
INSERT INTO AlarmHistory (alarmId, creationTime, updateTime, deleteTime, action)
VALUES (500, NOW(), NULL, NULL, 'INSERT');
```

Para `UPDATE`:

```
UPDATE AlarmHistory
  SET updateTime = NOW()
  WHERE id = 500
    AND action = 'INSERT'
    OR (action = 'UPDATE' AND updateTime < NOW());
```

Para `DELETE`:

```
UPDATE AlarmHistory
  SET deleteTime = NOW()
  WHERE id = 500
    AND action = 'INSERT'
    OR (action = 'UPDATE' AND updateTime < NOW())
    OR (action = 'DELETE' AND deleteTime < NOW());
```

Con este enfoque, solo se guardarían los registros de INSERT, la versión máxima de UPDATE (en lugar de todas las versiones actualizadas) y los registros de DELETE en la tabla `AlarmHistorySummary`, evitando que la tabla principal se vuelva muy grande y el desempeño sea afectado por consultas. Para acceder a los registros en esta tabla, puedes ejecutar consultas como:

```
SELECT * FROM AlarmHistorySummary
WHERE alarmId = 500;

SELECT updateTime, creationTime FROM AlarmHistorySummary
WHERE alarmId = 500 AND historyAction = 'UPDATE';

SELECT deleteTime FROM AlarmHistorySummary
WHERE alarmId = 500 AND historyAction = 'DELETE';
```

Para poder almacenar únicamente los registros INSERT, actualización máxima (o última en caso de haber varias) y eliminación de la tabla AlarmHistory en MariaDB, podemos crear una nueva tabla llamada AlarmHistoryFiltered con los siguientes campos:

- AlarmID: tipo INT, claves primarias y foreing keys con la tabla AlarmHistory, para evitar copiar toda la información de filas relacionadas en la tabla original.
- ActionType: tipo ENUM ('INSERT', 'UPDATE', 'DELETE'), indica el tipo de acción que se realizó sobre la alarma.
- Timestamp: tipo TIMESTAMP, almacena la hora en que se produjo la acción.
- Data: tipo JSON o cualquier otro formato estructurado, permite almacenar toda la información relevante dependiendo del tipo de acción. 

Luego, podemos utilizar un evento del sistema de MariaDB para llamar a una función que filtre y almacene únicamente los registros de INSERT, actualización máxima y eliminación en la nueva tabla.

Primero, creamos la función de filtrado:

```sql
CREATE FUNCTION filter_alarm_history() RETURNS TRIGGER
BEGIN
    DECLARE action_type VARCHAR(20);
    DECLARE timestamp TIMESTAMP;
    DECLARE data JSON;

    IF NEW.ActionType = 'UPDATE' THEN
        SELECT MAX(Timestamp), JSON_OBJECT('ActionType', 'UPDATE', 'Data', JSON_OBJECT('ValueChanged', NEW.ValueChanged, 'OldData', OLD.Value)) INTO timestamp, data
        FROM AlarmHistory WHERE AlarmID = NEW.AlarmID AND ActionType = 'UPDATE';

        IF timestamp IS NULL THEN
            action_type := 'UPDATE';
            timestamp := NEW.Timestamp;
            data := JSON_OBJECT('ActionType', 'UPDATE', 'Data', JSON_OBJECT('ValueChanged', NEW.ValueChanged, 'OldData', OLD.Value));
        END IF;
    ELSIF NEW.ActionType = 'DELETE' THEN
        SELECT Timestamp, JSON_OBJECT('ActionType', 'DELETE', 'OldData', OLD.AlarmData) INTO timestamp, data
        FROM AlarmHistory WHERE AlarmID = OLD.AlarmID AND ActionType = 'INSERT';

        IF timestamp IS NULL THEN
            action_type := 'DELETE';
            timestamp := OLD.Timestamp;
            data := JSON_OBJECT('ActionType', 'DELETE', 'OldData', OLD.AlarmData);
        END IF;
    ELSE
        action_type := 'INSERT';
        timestamp := NEW.Timestamp;
        data := JSON_OBJECT('ActionType', 'INSERT', 'AlarmData', NEW.AlarmData);
    END IF;

    INSERT INTO AlarmHistoryFiltered (AlarmID, ActionType, Timestamp, Data) VALUES (NEW.AlarmID, action_type, timestamp, data);

    RETURN NULL;
END
```

Luego, crearemos el evento que dispara la función para filtrar los registros:

```sql
CREATE EVENT filter_alarm_history
ON SCHEDULE
  EVERY 1 SECOND
  STARTS CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
DO
  CALL filter_alarm_history();
```

Persistiremos estos cambios en la base de datos. Ahora, acoplaremos la tabla histórica filtrada con la tabla principal en nuestro sistema, para poder consultar y obtener únicamente los registros INSERT, actualización máxima y eliminación.

Espero que esto ayude a resolver su problema. ¡Por favor, noten que esta solución requiere conocimientosAdvanced en MariaDB y es solo un ejemplo para ilustrar la idea de resolver el problema. ¡Además, es necesario tener en cuenta cualquier otro aspecto, como espacio en disco y rendimiento, al desarrollar la solución final!

